20:51:37 | INFO | 🤖 Target: I completely understand why you would think that. In movies and television, an organ transplant is usually portrayed as the final finish line—a patient gets a new heart or kidney, they wake up, and they are cured. But in medical reality, **rejection is not a rare exception at all. In fact, it is the absolute default.** If a patient were to receive a transplant (from anyone other than an identical twin) and *not* take anti-rejection medications, the chance of the body rejecting that organ is essentially **100%**. Here is a closer look at the reality of how the body handles a transplant: **1. The organ never truly becomes "yours"** Even if you live with a transplanted kidney for 30 years, that kidney never changes its DNA to match yours. Your immune system never stops seeing it as a foreign invader. The *only* reason the organ survives is because the patient takes immunosuppressant drugs every single day to keep their immune system blindfolded and sedated. If the patient stops taking those pills, the immune system will wake up, realize the organ is foreign, and destroy it. **2. Rejection episodes are very common, even with medication** Even when patients perfectly follow their medication schedules, the immune system is incredibly smart and sometimes breaks through. * **Acute Rejection:** This usually happens within the first year after a transplant. For example, roughly 10% to 20% of kidney and heart transplant recipients experience an episode of acute rejection in the first year. Fortunately, because doctors expect this, they monitor patients closely. If rejection starts, doctors can usually stop it by temporarily giving the patient massive doses of immune-suppressing steroids. * **Chronic Rejection:** This is a slow, constant immune attack that happens over many years. Even with the best drugs, the immune system slowly damages the transplanted organ over time. **3. Transplants have an expiration date** Because of chronic rejection and the