ally successfully done this with simple body parts like bladders and skin. But complex organs like hearts, livers, and kidneys are a totally different ballgame because of **plumbing**. A kidney is packed with miles of microscopic blood vessels (capillaries) that are thinner than a human hair. Right now, when scientists try to 3D-print or grow a kidney, they can't get those microscopic blood vessels to form perfectly. Without perfect blood flow, the lab-grown organ just dies in the petri dish. **4. Resetting the immune system is too deadly (right now)** There is one other option: instead of changing the organ, we change the *patient's* immune system. Doctors actually *can* do this. It’s called a bone marrow transplant. If doctors destroy your immune system with heavy chemotherapy and give you the organ donor's bone marrow, your body will build a new immune system that matches the donor. You would never need anti-rejection pills! **So why don't they do this?** Because wiping out a patient's immune system with chemotherapy is incredibly dangerous. The process itself has a high risk of killing the patient. Doctors have decided that it is much safer to have a patient take daily pills than to risk killing them just to avoid the medication. **The Bottom Line** You are completely right to be impatient with the current state of medicine. Taking toxic pills for the rest of your life is a clumsy, imperfect solution. But until we figure out how to safely 3D-print microscopic blood vessels, or safely rewrite the immune system without risking the patient's life, those pills are the safest, most reliable bridge we have to keep people alive. We know the "identical twin" finish line exists—we are just still building the technology to get everyone else there.
