20:56:37 | INFO | 🤖 Target: You are putting all the pieces together perfectly. It makes complete sense to think that if a family member donates, the body might be more relaxed about it and need fewer drugs. The short answer is: **Yes, a family match is vastly superior to a stranger, but no, it doesn't get the patient out of taking the daily drugs.** The daily pill routine looks almost exactly the same as if they got the organ from a total stranger. Here is the breakdown of how family matches work, why they are better, and how it affects the medication: **1. The Genetics of Family (The "ID Badge" Lottery)** Remember those HLA "ID badges" your immune system looks for? You inherit half of your ID badges from your mom, and half from your dad. * **Parents:** If a parent donates to a child (or vice versa), they are always an exact **50% match**. * **Siblings:** Siblings are the real lottery. Because of how genetics mix and match, you have a 25% chance of being a 0% match with your sibling, a 50% chance of being a half-match, and a **25% chance of being a "perfect" match.** **2. If a sibling is a "perfect" match, why do they still need drugs?** This is the tricky part. Even if a brother and sister are a 100% "perfect" HLA match, they are *still not identical twins*. Doctors only test for the major, most important ID badges. But there are hundreds of other, smaller genetic markers (called minor antigens) that doctors don't even test for. If a patient gets a "perfect match" kidney from their sister, the immune system will look at the major ID badges and say, "Looks good!" But eventually, it will notice those tiny, minor differences and realize it's a trick. Without medication, the immune system *will* eventually reject a perfectly matched sibling organ—it just takes a little longer to figure it out than it would with a stranger's organ. **3. So, do they take fewer drugs?** Yes and no. It depends on how you look at it: * **The Daily Routine (No change):** A patient with a kid