20:57:55 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That is an incredibly sharp observation. If the immune system never truly accepts the organ, it seems totally contradictory that doctors would lower the medication over time. You are actually touching on a really fascinating gray area in immunology. While the body never *fully* accepts the organ (which is why patients can never stop the drugs completely), the dynamic between the organ and the immune system **does change** over the years. Here is why doctors are able to safely lower the doses as time goes on: **1. The "Crime Scene" vs. Everyday Life** Right after a transplant surgery, the patient's body is in a state of absolute chaos. The surgeon just made a massive incision, moved organs around, and stitched blood vessels together. To the immune system, this surgical trauma looks like a massive, life-threatening attack. The immune system's alarms are blaring at maximum volume, and it sends millions of attack cells to the area. Because the immune system is hyper-aggressive right after surgery, doctors have to use massive doses of drugs to knock it down. But years later, the surgical wounds are completely healed. The alarms are off. The immune system is back to its normal, baseline resting state. Because it isn't in a state of hyper-panic anymore, it doesn't take nearly as much medication to keep it quiet. **2. The Organ Loses its "Red Flags"** When an organ is transplanted, it doesn't just bring the donor's kidney or heart cells; it also brings along some of the donor's own white blood cells (immune cells) hiding inside the blood vessels. Doctors call these "passenger cells." To the recipient's immune system, these passenger cells are like giant, waving red flags that scream, "WE ARE FOREIGN!" This triggers a fierce immune response. However, over the first few months and years, those donor passenger cells naturally die off. They are slowly replaced by the patient's *own* cells lining the inside of the organ's blood vessels. The organ stil