20:58:26 | INFO | 🤖 Target: You are making a brilliant point, and honestly, you have just reasoned your way into a concept that immunologists study very closely. It really *does* sound like a slow, gradual version of acceptance. In fact, in the medical world, doctors have a specific word for exactly what you are describing: **Accommodation**. But in biology, "Accommodation" is not the same thing as true "Acceptance" (which doctors call Tolerance). You are incredibly close to your original idea, but there is a harsh biological reality that separates the two. Here is why getting "used to it" isn't the same as truly accepting it: **1. The "Sleeping Bear" Reality** If the body truly, finally accepted the organ after 10 or 20 years of getting used to it, you would be able to stop taking the pills. But we know from tragic medical cases what happens if a patient stops their medication after 20 years: the immune system wakes up and destroys the organ, often within weeks. The immune system didn't learn to accept the organ; it was just acting like a sedated, sleeping bear. The medication was the tranquilizer dart. Over the years, it took less tranquilizer to keep the bear asleep, but the bear never stopped being a bear. If the tranquilizer wears off, it will attack just as fiercely as it would have on day one. **2. The Proof: "Slow-Motion" Rejection** This is the saddest proof that the body never truly accepts the organ: even when the immune system is "exhausted" and the patient is doing perfectly fine on low-dose medication, **the immune system is actually still attacking the organ the entire time.** It just switches from a fast, violent attack (Acute Rejection) to a slow, quiet attack (Chronic Rejection). Because the immune system can't blow the organ up, it slowly lays down scar tissue inside the organ's microscopic blood vessels. Year after year, this scar tissue builds up. It is like slowly pouring cement down a drain. This slow-motion attack is the exact reason why tran